{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "02 — Agent execution: the repair loop and its tools",
  "description": "The only code here that starts another program which writes code, plus the two tool surfaces: the read-only MCP server, and the after-the-fact check on what the agent actually called.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "src/cli.ts",
      "line": 519,
      "title": "Entry: proofloop codex-loop",
      "description": "Parses `--agent`, `--dry-run`, `--max-attempts`, `--run-id` and hands off. Errors here return exit 2 rather than throwing — a supervisor that crashes is worse than one that reports.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*async function runCodexLoopCommand\\(options: Record<string, string \\| boolean>, root: string\\): Promise<number> \\{\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/agentLoop.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "title": "The loop",
      "description": "Run the gate initially and after every successfully completed repair, including the final allowed repair. Only launch another agent when the gate still fails and the positive-integer launch budget has room. `attempts` counts launched processes, so an initially passing project reports zero.\n\nEach observation leaves `gate-attempt-N.json` in `.proofloop/runs/<run-id>/`, up to budget + 1 snapshots. A zero-exit repair is not itself a passing gate.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*while \\(true\\) \\{\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/agentLoop.ts",
      "line": 113,
      "title": "Gate result → agent verdict",
      "description": "The translation layer. Failed check names become `failedGates`; if the gate produced no state at all, the verdict still carries a reason (`gate_unavailable`) rather than an empty list. The agent is never told 'something failed' with nothing to act on.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*function verdictFromGate\\(runId: string, state: GateState \\| undefined, exitCode: number\\): ProofloopVerdict \\{\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/agentLoop.ts",
      "line": 97,
      "title": "When the agent cannot be launched",
      "description": "Dry run or no configured command: stop with zero launches. A launched process that exits nonzero counts once and also stops. Return the gate's failure code, with the prompt available for a human to act on. The launch budget does not add a wall-clock timeout to the existing adapter.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*if \\(options\\.dryRun \\|\\| !runResult\\.launched \\|\\| runResult\\.status === \"failed\"\\) \\{\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/mcp.ts",
      "line": 28,
      "title": "Tool registration",
      "description": "Five tools, a plain array — no framework, no decorators. Spoken over stdin/stdout as JSON-RPC (Model Context Protocol).\n\nThe boundary that matters: **every tool here is read-only**. An agent cannot use this server to change files.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*const TOOLS: McpTool\\[\\] = \\[\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/mcp.ts",
      "line": 56,
      "title": "The server loop",
      "description": "Newline-framed JSON-RPC over stdin. `runCli` returns the sentinel `MCP_SERVER_RUNNING` instead of an exit code so the process is not killed while the server is serving.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*export function startMcpServer\\(options: \\{ root: string \\}\\): void \\{\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/proofloopToolUse.ts",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Proving what the agent actually called",
      "description": "Read this header before changing anything in the file. The listed properties are load-bearing: an empty trace fails a required-tool contract instead of passing vacuously, and `mcp__evil__GMAIL_SEND_EMAIL` cannot satisfy a rule pinned to server `composio`.\n\nThe honest boundary is stated too: this proves what *this machine's* hooks observed, not what a provider recorded.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*/\\*\\*\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/proofloopToolUse.ts",
      "line": 519,
      "title": "The verifier",
      "description": "Deterministic, no model involved. Required tools (optionally pinned to one MCP server, with deep-subset parameter matching), forbidden tools (matched on both the full and bare name), and ordering rules.\n\nMalformed trace lines above 10% fail verification — corrupting the log cannot buy a pass.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*export function verifyToolUseContract\\(\\s*$"
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/fixtures/tooluse/composio-email-triage/contract.json",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "What a contract looks like",
      "description": "The fixture is the readable specification. Beside it are four traces — pass, forbidden call, missing required call, namespace spoof — and `tests/proofloopToolUse.test.ts` asserts the verdict for each.",
      "pattern": "^\\s*\\{\\s*$"
    }
  ]
}
